<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Aphaia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of Aphaia is a Doric temple on the island of Aegina, dedicated to a local goddess and built around 500 BCE. Its pediment sculptures, showing scenes of the Trojan wars, mark the transition from Archaic to Classical Greek sculpture. They were removed in 1811, bought by Crown Prince Ludwig of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glyptothek">Glyptothek</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-aphaia.jpg" alt="Temple of Aphaia" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paweł 'pbm' Szubert (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aegina_-_Temple_of_Aphaia_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
